.\n\nThen I aggregate across folds to decide architecture-level go/no-go, examining whether structural motifs or modules recurred across finalists and whether performance was stable. For the final DV25 model, I'm pre-registering two candidate approaches now: either refitting the identical frozen procedure on the full 117-stock universe to produce a frozen genome set validated by the DEV117 blind results, or using a cross-fold consensus/ensemble — leaning toward the full refit as primary, though I note that approach itself wouldn't be separately transport-tested.\n\nGiven multiple-testing concerns, I'd restrict DV25 to a small pre-declared candidate set — at most one candidate per target band, capped at two or three bands total — rather than burning DV25 on many options.\n\nDiagnostics like per-stock transport, era breakdowns, decline tables, and motif recurrence should only inform the go/no-go decision, never feed back into genome selection itself. If a redesign fails DEV117 gates and gets iterated again, that's another look that erodes the blind set's validity, so I should cap further iterations — maybe two more looks at most — and log each one.\n\nRanking discrepancies now, the most critical one: the global linear score converted to cross-sectional rank makes all market and R1 features mathematically inert for selection, since rank is invariant to per-date constants, and it fixes the number of qualifying names by quantile regardless of exposure state. This single design choice could eliminate crash-defense, R1 discovery, regime-conditioned behavior, and SAFE competition — likely explaining why SAFE scores near zero.\n\nChecking further: if strength is derived as rank minus qualification threshold and weights are gross-normalized to ≤1, the portfolio stays fully invested whenever any strength is positive, meaning SAFE margin is either always or never binding against a rank-based distribution — so it essentially never triggers. I'm also flagging the absence of any sector layer, and the absence of any strategy-family, applicability, or conditional structure, since a single global linear model can't represent that kind of conditional logic.\n\nThat means SAFE isn't a genuine competitor at all — it's a direct consequence of the rank-scoring setup, with measured SAFE converging near 1/T. The turnover/SAFE metric landing near 1/5030 looks suspicious too, almost identical across runs, which points to either a metric artifact or an initialization-only event; I need to root-cause whether this reflects a \"day-one basket\" degeneracy where the GA never replaces its initial picks, effectively doing stock selection via ticker identity rather than learned signal. I'm also noting the comparator mislabels SPY as the equal-weight mean of DEV117, which may improperly include the blind37 stocks.\n\nI need to confirm this isn't leaking into any feature or decline-episode definition used by evolution, since that would make the reporting invalid. The cost model is a flat hard-coded 5bps with no sensitivity testing and no regulatory or borrow costs, and given near-zero training turnover, cost pressure barely matters anyway. Equal allocation was omitted entirely, which is a spec violation with moderate scientific consequence since equal weighting is often a robust baseline. I'm also flagging that stock features are purely cross-sectional ranks with no time-series or absolute state, making time-varying conviction impossible — combined with the earlier design flaw, this bumps up to a high-severity issue. There's also unresolved provenance questions around the market feature's universe, possible cross-partition contamination, and short-selling mechanics that need auditing.\n\nI'm noting the short mechanics use a symmetric boolean inverse-rank scheme that forces an always-on short book with no borrow costs or state-conditioned applicability. Finalist selection via blind band display risks selection bias since whichever candidate appears in the blind frontier gets chosen, which needs pre-registration to fix. I suspect required diagnostics like CVaR5, drawdown velocity, and effective N may be missing from the reporting packet and should be flagged as unverified until tested, while the search budget itself seems adequate and the fixed 8-12 feature count is a moderate concern since it was meant to be provisional, not a permanent ceiling.\n\nGiven that 20 of 28 features turned out inert, it looks like the GA wasted slots on dead weight, though the simplifications like single-file implementation, vectorized rank transforms, causal percentile transforms, and deterministic folds are all reasonable choices. Now I'm cataloguing genuine ambiguities in the approved spec itself — how SAFE competes in units, the friction-vs-rate tradeoff, market breadth universe provenance, cross-fold finalist selection rules, the absence of numeric economic advancement thresholds, drawdown band semantics, training-versus-blind universe size asymmetry, execution timing, and data availability for earnings and sector ETFs.\n\nI also need to flag data-availability issues: VVIX history effectively starts around 2007, so era 1 starting in 2006-09 would be missing that feature, and OFR funding/credit series have varying start dates too — this needs explicit handling for missing features before 2007. Separately, the disappointing economic results (8.85 vs 19.86) are a valid negative result for the reduced runner but don't actually test the approved architecture, and the fact that blind CAGR exceeds training at the 20% candidate suggests partition beta differences rather than real skill, reinforcing that the selection diagnostic shows no genuine selection skill.\n\nI'm also weighing whether CAGR/MDD is the right Pareto objective — MDD is a single-path, high-variance statistic dominated by the GFC episode in training, which incentivizes overfitting to that one crisis; I'll keep CAGR/MDD as the frozen objective but add guards like era-specific MDD and leave-one-episode-out validation. On whether linear rank is fundamentally too weak for this architecture, I conclude yes — not because linear models can't capture alpha, but because rank-invariance strips out context, there's no way to model conditional interactions or absolute levels, and the quantile qualification step artificially constrains investment breadth.\n\nA linear model with interactions and absolute output is really just a special case of the richer genome space, so I should seed it as a baseline module to ensure the new GA's hypothesis space strictly contains the old one — a nesting property I can verify with a regression test confirming the old finalists are reproducible within tolerance. For the high-dimensional conditional search problem, I'm considering typed GP with modules, soft applicability gating (sigmoid/fuzzy), mixture-of-experts, NEAT-style complexification with speciation, MAP-Elites, module libraries with crossover and caching, optional surrogate-assisted screening, and hierarchical co-evolution where modules are evaluated in portfolio context with differentiable gates mutated by the GA.\n\nChecking data availability for earnings surprises and point-in-time data — if this isn't available, that's a hard incompatibility requiring approval before proceeding, same issue applies to sector ETF histories for sectors that didn't exist historically. I'm drafting a preflight checklist (PF-01 through PF-30) with machine-testable pass criteria, plus requirements for independent conformance audits, a spec traceability matrix, and a freeze manifest covering code/config/data hashes, seeds, feature registry, cost model, and decision rules. Given the scope, this response needs to be long and well-structured using tables and bullets; I'm also sanity-checking the OmniFunds return claims — a 10-11%/month rate compounds to absurd multi-billion-fold returns over 20 years, which is a red flag worth flagging explicitly.\n\nI'm now picking apart each strategy's claimed performance: Market Leaders' 86.2% YTD is actually mostly backtest multiplied by a thin 5-week post-release period, which is statistically meaningless and likely just reflects 2026 AI-sector tailwinds baked into the universe selection itself; Model 2 claims \"40% since release\" but omits a release date, making it impossible to annualize or trust.\n\nWhat I take from these are testable hypotheses rather than direct imports — concentration in leaders is already allowed under MTS, a market-level cash brake could work better as a discovered risk-appetite signal than a hardcoded rule, and the semis drawdown exposes a real need to measure sector concentration and worst-day risk. I also flag that the universe difference (NASDAQ-100 vs. the unknown composition of DEV117) matters a lot for ceiling comparisons, but changing universes to match isn't something governance allows.\n\nI'm now working through effective sample size details: era and fold stability via CAGR dispersion, cluster-robust and HAC methods for daily observations with bootstrap block length chosen by autocorrelation, counting distinct market episodes rather than days, and computing effective numbers of contributing stocks, independent bets, and independent trials for deflation purposes.\n\nNoting that fold overlap (4×37 vs 117) means folds aren't truly independent, so I need wide confidence intervals via stationary block bootstrap and stock-resampling for CAGR/MDD. I'm sketching proposed guard thresholds for training — validity constraints on gross exposure and leverage, economic significance versus SAFE CAGR and passive benchmarks, per-era performance floors, tail-loss bands, maximum underwater duration, and evidence concentration checks via leave-top-3 analysis.\n\nNow I'm adding more guards: inner-resample stability across K=8 subsets, and cost stress testing at 2× baseline. I want to use constrained domination where infeasible solutions rank below feasible ones, sorted by violation severity so evolution can still climb. I'm checking that the exposure-matched passive benchmark doesn't unfairly penalize crash-defense strategies, and confirming the cost model and turnover diagnostics catch high-turnover pathologies.\n\nNow I'm drafting the behavioral band thresholds and the L-level conformance table, starting with L1 (full PF gates plus independent audit) and L2 (primary economic floor requiring median CAGR ≥15% across folds, minimum 12% per fold, and MDD staying within the 25% band in most folds).\n\nFor the finer-grained bands I'm scaling CAGR requirements with drawdown tolerance: 12% at ≤15% MDD up through 23% at ≤40% MDD, while keeping a minimum CAGR/|MDD| ratio around 0.55-0.8 across bands, checking these against the current performance baseline.\n\nEvery band at 25%, 30%, 35%, and 40% MDD fails the required ratio, though a prior 50-stock test at 26%/37% did pass (but that's dev-set evidence I'm not counting). My reasoning rests on two things: the strategy needs to clearly beat passive SPY returns to justify its complexity and model risk, and I should expect a 30-50% haircut when going from the development sample to live performance.\n\nDeriving the required floor as live_target divided by (1 minus haircut), if I want ≥10% live CAGR at ≤20-25% DD and assume a 33% haircut, that works out to roughly 15% blind. I also want to require that at least one of the 20/25/30 MDD bands pass, not just the 40% band.\n\nFor the full validation ladder, I'm adding benchmark dominance tests — blind CAGR beating SPY total return by at least 4pp with MDD under 0.6x SPY's, outperforming exposure-matched SPY/T-bill blends by 5pp, and beating buy-and-hold on the universe at lower drawdown. Then statistical layers: stationary block bootstrap lower bounds on excess CAGR, deflated Sharpe ratios, and PBO checks via CSCV to guard against overfitting.\n\nI'm also building selection-skill checks — comparing selected versus unselected names with HAC-adjusted significance, requiring positive results across most folds — plus era-level consistency requirements so no single period drags the strategy below acceptable thresholds, and crash-period tests requiring losses capped relative to SPY during major drawdowns with faster recovery times.\n\nFor costs, I want performance to survive at 10bps with a buffer at 20bps, and borrow-cost sensitivity shouldn't flip the pass/fail if shorts carry too much weight. On the evidence side, I'm setting diversification requirements so no single stock or episode dominates the blind PnL.\n\nFor structural recurrence I want the passing finalists' motifs to hold up individually across most folds, not just in aggregate. And for SAFE usage, I'm not forcing the GA to actually use SAFE — it just needs to be a legitimate, available candidate; shorts-only strategies can still pass if they're genuinely better.\n\nFor DV25, I'm thinking the decision rule should allow a pre-registered tolerance below the full L2 floors — say 80% of the floor threshold — while still requiring it to beat SPY and stay within drawdown bounds, run over the same 20-year window with 25 fresh stocks, before A25 gets triggered.\n\nExpected value matters too: if DEV117 only marginally passes, I'd rather not burn DV25 unless its predicted pass probability is at least 60%, estimated by bootstrapping 25-stock subsets from the blind37 fold results to see what fraction would satisfy the DV25 rule. Now I'm turning to the turnover anomaly — my first hypothesis is a metric artifact where the turnover calculation in the training path is picking up the wrong weight array.\n\nThe matching values between the turnover figure and the SAFE fraction (both roughly 1/T) suggest they're actually measuring the same thing — likely both capturing just the initial allocation session. My second hypothesis is population degeneracy: if the replacement margin has evolved too strict, positions never rotate after initial entry, so turnover only fires once at the start, which naturally produces 1/T whether it's the SAFE fraction or the average turnover.\n\nBut on blind data turnover rises to 0.03-0.07, which doesn't fit pure buy-and-hold. With 37 stocks, this could be forced churn from delistings or missing data compelling exits, or rank volatility crossing the replacement margin more often out-of-sample than in-sample. If this holds, training fitness essentially reflects a hindsight-selected static basket rather than a genuinely adaptive trading strategy, which would be a serious overfitting flag for the genetic algorithm.\n\nIf confirmed, this means the GA's weights are tuned so the first-day cross-sectional rank effectively picks the best-performing training stocks over the 20-year window — a hindsight leakage mechanism disguised as ticker-identity encoding via feature vectors, which would be critical if true, and would also explain the weak blind-selection diagnostics. I want to check why training CAGR isn't higher given this hindsight advantage, though the max-drawdown constraint could be suppressing it. I'm also wondering whether era resets restart the portfolio from a safe state, which would change the expected safe-fraction ratio, and whether the displayed 1/T value implies continuous operation rather than resets. I plan to dump the daily weight series along the training path to test this directly.\n\nThe real countermeasure against a static day-one basket is to require robustness across staggered start dates — if fitness is evaluated with multiple shifted training start offsets and a genome's performance depends on ranking stocks from day one, it should fail when the start date shifts, since a legitimately adaptive strategy wouldn't rely on the original day-one feature ordering. This \"start-date jitter\" test, combined with diagnostics like tracking the fraction of PnL coming from positions held since session one, seems like the right way to catch hindsight selection without banning legitimately low-turnover genomes.\n\nI also note that era resets effectively create four separate day-one baskets rather than one, and that an unusually high blind-period CAGR relative to training could simply reflect that the 37 blind stocks had stronger drift — in a buy-and-hold scenario, blind performance would just track the blind basket's beta. A negative selected-minus-unselected gap would indicate anti-skill, where the \"CAGR\" is coming from general equity drift rather than stock-picking ability.\n\nTurning to open questions in the spec that need resolution before freezing it, I'm compiling a list of unspecified items — things like the missing numeric economic target, SAFE competition units, friction/rate exchange assumptions, market-feature universe provenance, finalist selection rules, execution timing, short collateral/rebate terms, point-in-time sector taxonomy, and data availability gaps (earnings, VVIX, sector data) — all of which left room for the kind of reduction I'm worried about.\n\nI also want to flag a governance concern: since I've already seen DEV117's blind results from this reduced run and I'm using those anomalies in my review, DEV117 blind is no longer fully \"virgin\" — it still works as a transport test, but future advancement decisions need DV25 as well, since DV25 remains sealed. I'll make sure not to expose any protected DV25/A25/A75/B100 evidence.\n\nNow I'm drafting the typed genome schema, starting with the context layer structure for market and sector context units...\n\nStill writing schema...\n\nI'm also laying out arithmetic primitives like safe division and clipping, plus fuzzy logic operators for AND/OR/NOT and soft thresholding via sigmoid functions.\n\nNow I'm sketching the StrategyModule structure itself—fields for a module's family tag, an applicability gate that scores market/sector/stock context, a signed signal expression, a side mode restricting long/short behavior, a module weight, and an uncertainty estimate.\n\nI'm defining the scalar type hierarchy—market-level, sector-level, and stock-level values—along with promotion rules so market scalars can broadcast down to stocks, and noting that a signal must contain at least one stock-varying term so modules can actually differentiate between stocks for ranking purposes.\n\nI'm also sketching the aggregator modes (weighted sum, weighted mean, max-confidence, softmax) and the opportunity map, which converts contextual expressions into expected excess returns either through a simple affine transform or a causal-calibrated approach using realized historical returns over the current tradable universe.\n\nBeyond that, I'm defining the lifecycle gene (friction coefficients, uncertainty penalties, no-trade bands, exit behavior), the allocation gene (sizing modes like equal, strength, uncertainty, or downside-weighted, with a risk appetite parameter and sharpness control), and now I'm specifying the short gene, including applicability conditions and always-on borrow-cost awareness.\n\nI'm also setting bounds on complexity -- generous engineering limits like max modules, depth, expression size, and active concepts -- with a rule to pause and request approval if elites start saturating any limit, since that would artificially constrain the hypothesis space. And I'm defining a registry of lookback windows for temporal operations, treating these strictly as measurement windows rather than holding horizons.\n\nI'm working out mutation and crossover operators (homologous module crossover via innovation IDs, type-safe subtree crossover, context-unit exchange, layer swaps), plus a strict rejection pipeline for invalid individuals: schema check, forbidden-symbol check, causality/lag check, bounds check, a cheap probe evaluation, then feasibility checks on weights -- all logged and counted before any full simulation runs. Finally I'm setting up deduplication via canonicalization, structural hashing, and behavioral fingerprinting.\n\nFor dedup, I'm deciding that exact structural hash matches can skip full re-evaluation safely, but behavioral fingerprint matches (computed on a probe calendar subset including crash periods) only flag near-duplicates for diversity/niching purposes since subset equality doesn't guarantee full-period equivalence -- true full-period weight-hash equality gets checked post-evaluation for archive dedup instead. I'm also noting that phenotypic equivalence cases like scaling by a positive constant before soft-rank get caught by canonicalization. Then I'm starting to lay out the island structure for Section H: 8 islands per fold, splitting between unconstrained NSGA-II islands and epsilon-constraint islands across drawdown bands from 10 to 40.\n\nI'm settling on 9 islands total per fold: seven band-specific islands each maximizing CAGR subject to a max drawdown constraint, one global multi-objective island, and one novelty/MAP-Elites island, giving 2,250 evaluations per generation. Generation budgets scale across stages (100/150/250) for a base of 500 with plateau-based stopping extending to 800 under a pre-registered rule, and I'm estimating unique evaluations per fold at roughly 0.5-1.2M after caching, translating to 14-55 CPU-hours per fold given vectorized numpy evaluation speeds -- feasible on multi-core hardware without needing to shrink the hypothesis space. Now I'm thinking through migration timing between islands, starting with every 25 generations.\n\nFor migration I'm specifying ring-topology transfer of the top 5% with re-evaluation, where band islands receive migrants under constrained selection, and I'm defining novelty via behavior descriptors like exposure, SAFE levels, short share, turnover, holding concentration, and crash/recovery performance feeding a MAP-Elites archive. I'm also setting stagnation rules -- hypervolume improvement below 0.5% over 40 generations triggers random immigrant injection and increased mutation, with two consecutive stagnations freezing an island and reallocating its compute -- and laying out four evolutionary stages that progressively unlock contexts, full lifecycle/allocation/short co-evolution, and finally robustness refinement with guards fully enforced.\n\nI'm now reconsidering whether guards should be enforced from the very start or loosened early via pre-registered progressive constraint tightening, while clarifying that staging only adjusts operator probabilities and constraint strictness rather than removing any gene types. I'm defining a deterministic seeding scheme (master seed hashed by fold/island/gen) with RNG states checkpointed, requiring checkpoints every 10 generations to be hash-verified and reproducible on resume, and I'm working through what data the training process is actually allowed to see -- per-stock features and labels, external market features, SAFE, and whether raw SPY values count as an input feature versus just a reporting comparator.\n\nI'm flagging that market features like mret5/mret20/mdd need a clear, legitimate data source rather than being derived from the same proxy array used for reporting, since that risks leaking blind-partition information, and recommending they instead come from real SPY/broad-index data and a certified external universe -- this needs explicit user approval since it changes feature definitions. I'm also specifying the actual 3-month Treasury rate source as FRED's DTB3 series, noting its daily publication schedule via the H.15 release.\n\nFor further rigor, I note FRED values are dated for the observation date but released the next business day, so I'm recommending a one-session lag and the ALFRED vintage for point-in-time accuracy, plus the formula to convert discount basis to investment yield with daily accrual including weekends. I'm also detailing SPY data needs -- total-return dividend-adjusted pricing verified against independent checkpoints with a stored hash, alongside price-only SPY for transparency -- and listing the regulatory cost components: SEC Section 31 fees, FINRA TAF per-share caps, and borrow fee assumptions with sensitivity ranges for easy-to-borrow versus hard-to-borrow scenarios.\n\nNow I'm thinking through short-selling mechanics: no short rebate baseline, locate/recall risk as a diagnostic factor, and the need to ensure short dividends are paid correctly so total-return short positions equal the negative of the long total return. For execution timing, I'm weighing decision-at-close-T versus execution-at-T+1-open (needing open price data) against a more conservative T+1-close execution if only close prices are available, plus noting that intraday stop-losses can't be modeled without intraday fills. Finally I'm starting to lay out the realistic cost model during evolution, estimating half-spread by liquidity tier using high/low/close-based spread estimators.\n\nNow I'm flagging a potential conflict: the realistic cost model during evolution versus the flat \"0/5/10/20 bps\" sensitivity levels used at the finalist stage — these could mean either add-ons to the baseline or total replacement cost levels, which is ambiguous and needs clarification. I'm leaning toward treating those bps levels as total one-way spread+slippage costs (with commission still zero, regulatory and borrow added separately), reporting the realistic liquidity-based model as an additional column, and flagging this definition as something to confirm with the user.\n\nSince I can't actually pause to ask, I'm noting this as an open ambiguity to resolve at freeze time, with a default proposal stated. Now I'm shifting to drafting the gating criteria list — starting with traceability (every frozen requirement must map to code and test), layer existence (confirming all genome layers like MarketContext, SectorContext, StockState, StrategyModule, Opportunity ranking, and Allocation actually instantiate), and anti-inertness checks (verifying that perturbing each layer's inputs produces a measurable change in target weights, especially catching cases where market-level signals fail to affect gross exposure).\n\nI'm continuing to spell out these gates: testing against rank-cancellation so market features actually influence outcomes, validating sector context handling (point-in-time maps, feature influence, avoiding direct sector one-hot encoding), confirming the four allocation modes are all selectable and produce correct fixture weights, verifying SPY data integrity against hashed source files with correlation/difference checks against EW-DEV, and ensuring SPY and other comparators aren't leaking into GA inputs unless explicitly registered.\n\nI'm also laying out cost sensitivity checks (fixed cost levels matching hand-calculated net returns, monotonicity), regulatory/borrow mechanics (fees on sells only, daily borrow accrual, short dividend payments), strict sealed data isolation at the OS level with canary files and universe hash verification against DEV117, and now moving into fold integrity checks starting with fold file hashing.\n\nPer-fold I need train/blind sets to be disjoint, both subsets of DEV117, and the evolution process for each fold to only have access to its training data—any attempt to touch the blind set should raise an error. For blind write-only enforcement, blind results go to a location evolution processes can't read, and blind evaluation only happens once all four folds' finalists are frozen with timestamps and hashes. I'm also specifying a causality time-travel test confirming truncated-data features exactly match full-data features at any given time, and a publication lag check ensuring rates reflect only data published by that timestamp.\n\nNow I'm adding checks for forward-leaking labels (inaccessible to live computation), forbidding hold-time variables like age or cooldown periods via static scan and entry-date invariance tests, disallowing session cooldowns and position/sector caps so concentration can reach 100% when warranted, and verifying leverage stays bounded with no options instruments permitted.\n\nI'm also specifying SAFE-asset fixtures (ensuring SAFE allocation rises appropriately with negative expectations or yield increases, with unbiased initialization), lifecycle labeling correctness for ENTER/EXIT/REPLACE decisions, ticker-identity invariance under permutation/renaming, and unit tests for each performance metric against hand-computed references.\n\nNow I'm adding cross-path consistency checks (training vs blind path must match exactly, with sentinel alerts for degenerate turnover/SAFE patterns), era vs continuous calendar handling, full determinism under fixed seeds regardless of worker count, and a nesting regression test confirming the new schema can reproduce the old runner's finalist genomes within tolerance — while being careful to account for known bugs like the turnover artifact in the old runner.\n\nI'm also outlining expressiveness checks (each strategy family needs working seed templates, conditionals, temporal ops), data conformance rules (point-in-time earnings, documented missing-data handling for things like pre-2007 VVIX), engineering-limit monitoring, decline reporting on fixtures with real SPY data, survivorship documentation for constituent selection and delisting returns, and finally a config-freeze mechanism hashing config, code, data, and registry together.\n\nThe blocking rule is that the runner's main function calls preflight checks first, and any failure forces a nonzero exit before evolution starts, with overrides only possible via a signed approval file that itself becomes a new frozen spec version. I'm sketching the MUST/MAY/MUST NOT directive concisely, and thinking about adding a compact index mapping the 18 questions to the lettered output sections for usability. Now I'll start writing the full document with headings, aiming for precision without bloat.\n\nI'm working out the opportunity/excess return formula for a session, then the long and short net-value formulas accounting for uncertainty penalties and borrowing costs, carefully reasoning through how a short position forgoes the safe rate on encumbered equity.\n\nSubstituting the excess return definition, I find shorts effectively must overcome twice the safe rate under a no-rebate assumption, which seems like a noteworthy result worth double-checking against how collateral and SAFE earnings interact across long, short, and remaining equity.\n\nFor longs, moving a dollar from SAFE to the position is cleaner: the excess is simply the expected return spread. Then for replacement decisions — swapping from position i to j — I'm setting up the condition that the gain in expected value must exceed the combined exit and entry costs plus some risk/urgency penalty term, with a threshold scalar governing when replacement is worthwhile.\n\nI'm considering whether that threshold should be allowed to go to zero, letting any marginal improvement trigger replacement, but that risks excessive churn since costs still get charged against fitness. I'm deciding to let it range over a bounded interval, treating it purely as a friction exchange rate rather than an implied holding horizon — a skeptic could read it as forcing a minimum duration, but I want to clarify it's just an amortization hurdle that never actually constrains how long a position can be held.\n\nNow I'm working out the mechanics of rebalancing toward target weights: computing desired weights, then only executing trades per asset where the estimated value gain clears the friction hurdle, with a no-trade band for marginal cases. I'm also thinking through funding source logic — whether new positions draw from cash reserves or from closing other positions — via a greedy algorithm that ranks candidate trades by net value gain and executes the profitable ones subject to exposure limits.\n\nSAFE itself serves as the numeraire, with V=0 and zero uncertainty. For risk appetite τ, each asset's claim scales with τ times a mode-dependent qualification function—in equal mode, assets qualifying above some margin m each receive equal weight τ-scaled.\n\nFor strength mode, higher γ concentrates exposure rather than spreading it evenly. I'm also considering whether τ should respond to portfolio-state signals like current gross exposure, concentration, or average pairwise correlation—these are causal measurements of the book itself, not equity drawdown, so I'll flag book-composition features as recommended pending explicit approval since they weren't in the original registered list.\n\nSince adding isn't the same as narrowing the frozen spec, I can propose this as an extension requiring explicit user sign-off at freeze time—if unapproved, it's simply omitted without narrowing anything. I'm also noting that crash-state detection could use each R1 dimension as a separate typed market feature combined with temporal operators like velocity, acceleration, and persistence.\n\nI want a mandatory preflight test (PF-33) where I plant a recovery-like composite signal in synthetic data and verify the GA can find it within budget across enough seeds—a genuine power test for discoverability. I'd pair this with a negative control: running the GA on pure noise returns to confirm no spurious transport occurs between training and blind frontiers.\n\nI also want a null-distribution run to calibrate how much overfitting to expect by chance: running the full GA on block-permuted or synthetic no-predictability returns, measuring the blind performance it achieves, and using that as a principled bar. This feeds into the gate logic—L should require blind performance to exceed the 95th percentile of that null-run blind frontier.\n\nI need to be careful that this null run uses only the dev data, leaving the sealed set untouched. The 10% exposure band produced nothing useful since constant full exposure makes MDD below ~15% impossible, which is why the new design needs a SAFE mode instead.\n\nLooking at the GFC evidence, I want to make sure features like gap20, gap50, slope20, slope50, positive20, and breadth20/200 delta are included as individual substrate components at the market level, rather than only the derived composites like failed_rebound_reduction or prior_destruction that the runner currently aggregates.\n\nI also need to give the GA these dimensions separately per spec so it can discover interactions itself, while keeping composites clearly labeled as derived measurements rather than hand-coded rules — especially checking whether something like price_impulse is actually a hand-tuned binary flag with thresholds calibrated on the full DEV117 history, which would make it both a hidden rule and potentially hindsight-tuned on blind data. This raises a deeper question: were the R1 composite definitions and their thresholds originally tuned using the full DEV117 set or broader market history? If so, tuning on SPY market history from 2006-2026 would introduce era-level leakage rather than partition leakage, since market-level features are shared across all stocks and the evaluation period overlaps with that history.\n\nComposites built around known recovery episodes like 2009 or 2020 would carry hindsight baked in from the same market path being evaluated, which is a real research-design flaw — it can't be fully eliminated, but I can mitigate it by using generic, untuned constituent primitives, documenting the provenance of each composite, and running ablation tests comparing results with and without the composites included. I also realize that DV25, despite introducing fresh stocks, likely shares the same time window, so cross-stock validation tests stock-selection generalization but never actually tests market-timing or crash-logic hindsight, since every partition shares the same handful of market episodes regardless of which stocks are held out.\n\nI'm thinking through mitigations like leave-one-episode-out evaluation of market-context components, requiring improvements across multiple episodes rather than just one, and being upfront that true validation of market-timing logic really requires live, forward-looking data beyond the current cutoff — this feels like an important limitation to flag clearly in the review. I'm also weighing whether to propose a walk-forward era holdout given that the eras are currently treated as evaluation periods rather than training splits.\n\nI'm noting that prior morphology research drew on the same GFC/2020 episodes, so those composite features may already carry hindsight bias from that overlapping history. Now I'm sketching the overall structure for the writeup: a short executive verdict up front, then root cause analysis covering the verdict on the research question, a ranked discrepancy table, likely bugs, a classification of design gaps versus implementation defects versus disappointing results, and inherent limitations — aiming to keep the whole thing crisp despite the large scope.\n\nI'm also laying out the remaining sections after root cause: research architecture, genetic representation, feature substrate, lifecycle, portfolio construction, fitness, evolution schedule, DEV117 validation, economic realism, preflight gates, advancement criteria, and finally the directive, which needs to close out the document. I'm thinking a short question-to-section map should go near the top, right after the verdict, rather than at the end.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is a large, complex request requiring a thorough review across multiple sections plus a final directive, while keeping governance authoritative and not weakening any requirements or opening sealed data. I'm also sanity-checking OmniFunds' claims by computing the implied annualized return from a 10-11% monthly rate—roughly 214-250% per year once compounded over twelve months.\n\nExtending that math over twenty years produces an absurd multiple, making the durability and capacity claims implausible. I'm also cross-checking Market Leaders' performance figures—86.2% YTD built on a 70.8% backtest plus a post-release 9% gain over about five weeks—and confirming the compounding is internally consistent, though annualizing a single month's 9% gain to ~152% is statistically meaningless. I note Model 2's \"40% since release\" claim lacks a release date or denominator, and flag that its 5-day fixed holding period in the demo directly violates the MTS prohibition on fixed holding periods, which becomes a key architectural distinction not to import.\n\nNow I'm cataloguing other architectural gaps: no concentration cap, no cash brake equivalent, no sector/leadership layer, and potential survivorship or selection bias from using a 2026 NASDAQ-100 universe during a year when AI leaders happened to surge. The most critical issue I'm flagging is that the hierarchical market→sector→stock architecture has been collapsed into a single linear score, and because market-level features are identical across all stocks on a given day, they get added as a constant to every score—meaning cross-sectional ranking mathematically cancels them out entirely, rendering the market layer inert.\n\nThis strongly implies the rank-based qualification always selects a fixed fraction of stocks regardless of market conditions, which would make gross exposure effectively constant near 100% every day, the SAFE brake never actually triggering, and the SAFE margin never binding since long strength from rank is always positive. I want to flag this as a likely design flaw that needs verification against the actual implementation, since the packet only describes combining features linearly into a score and then ranking cross-sectionally.\n\nI notice something odd: training turnover ≈ 0.0001988 exactly matches the SAFE fraction. That's suspicious — checking the math, 1/5030 ≈ 0.0001988, and 20 years of trading sessions is roughly 5030 days, so this looks like both metrics are accidentally computing the same 1/N_sessions quantity rather than two distinct measures.\n\nIf the turnover figure is literally just one trade spread over the whole run, that implies the training portfolio essentially never rebalanced — which seems implausible with daily rank-based allocation unless the turnover calculation itself is broken, perhaps computed only once on the initial SAFE weight vector instead of tracking actual position changes.\n\nThat 1/5030 figure clicks — SAFE was held for exactly one session, the initial warmup day before signals kicked in, so that fraction is purely an initialization artifact rather than a meaningful trading pattern. Likewise the training turnover of 1/5030 implies replacement never actually fired after deployment — the GA essentially froze on its first-day selection, which raises the question of whether it stumbled onto a buy-and-hold basket that happened to work well simply because it was chosen on day one.\n\nThis points to a potential leakage path: since outcomes across the training partition feed back as labels, the GA could tune its day-one ranking weights to implicitly pick stocks that turned out to be winners over the full twenty-year training window — essentially a disguised form of hindsight bias through ticker selection. But that raises a puzzle for the blind set: if it's the same genome just picking different stocks there, why would turnover be higher? I can't pin that down yet, so I want to lay out several hypotheses to test, and also nail down exactly how turnover is defined — one-way or two-way, whether it includes the SAFE leg, and whether it's computed before or after normalization.\n\nI also notice the selection diagnostic is negative in the blind set — the top 20% candidates underperform next-period returns — yet CAGR is still positive, meaning the gains are coming from simply being invested (beta) rather than from the ranking model's selection skill. Oddly, the blind CAGR (8.85%) actually beats the training CAGR (4.95%), suggesting the blind 37-stock partition just has a stronger drift/beta, not better selection — I should compare against an equal-weight benchmark of those same 37 stocks and against SPY to isolate this. I confirm the blind ending multiple of about 5.43x matches the compounded 8.85% CAGR over twenty years. I also flag that the hard-coded 5bps cost assumption is a high-priority issue since near-zero training turnover means transaction costs never really pressured the training process.\n\nBeyond that, I'm noting that equal allocation was omitted entirely, which is a spec violation of medium scientific consequence, and that the SPY comparator used for reporting is fake — a high-priority issue for validity even if it's not wired into fitness. I need to check whether market features like 5/20-day returns, drawdown, and breadth are computed from the full 117-stock matrix including the blind 37 — if so, even though these are causal (using only past data), the spec explicitly prohibits any outcome from the transport partition entering evolution for that fold, so this needs careful scrutiny.\n\nI'm flagging the breadth universe provenance as an audit item: if breadth features are computed from a universe that includes sealed DV25/A25 stocks, that's reading sealed data directly, which is worse than mild contamination. The fix would be to source market-level features from external, non-sealed data like actual SPY prices, sector ETFs, or published NYSE advance/decline figures rather than deriving them from the dev or sealed universes.\n\nThis is more subtle contemporaneous leakage rather than future leakage — breadth features at time T reflect whether blind stocks are above their moving averages, correlating with blind-stock paths even though fitness doesn't directly touch blind returns. The transport-validity risk is modest in magnitude, but governance standards still require flagging it.\n\nFor sector classification, I'm worried about using sector one-hot encoding as a near-identity feature with only 80 stocks since some sectors may have just 2-3 members — better to feed in sector-level measurements like sector ETF returns or stock-relative-to-sector performance rather than raw sector labels, or restrict sector labels to grouping purposes only using point-in-time classifications.\n\nFor sector ETFs, XLK and XLF-style funds go back to 1998, but newer ones like XLC and XLRE only start in 2015/2018, so I need a plan for missing sector coverage — likely using sector ETFs as the primary signal and peer-stock aggregates as a backup with reliability weighting, ensuring the held-out blind stocks still get valid sector-relative features computed from the external ETF data so the approach transfers properly.\n\nWith 117 total development stocks and only 80 for training, I'm thinking through effective sample size concerns, which leads me to sketch the genome structure: a set of soft context detectors for market and sector regimes, a collection of strategy modules each tagged by family with gated applicability and signal/uncertainty/downside expressions, plus an aggregation layer to combine them.\n\nFor the opportunity estimator, I need to work out how to define expected return without committing to a fixed horizon — framing it as a one-session-ahead incremental edge so holding duration emerges naturally rather than being fixed upfront, while still allowing forward trajectories as discovery labels for fitness evaluation.\n\nThe GA itself doesn't actually need labels since fitness comes directly from simulated equity curves. The real challenge is calibration: scores must be comparable to SAFE's yield, so the opportunity score needs a causal, expanding-window calibration on training stocks that converts raw scores into expected next-session excess return over SAFE, using only information available up to the prior session.\n\nThis calibration approach carries over into blind evaluation too — the frozen genome can calibrate against the blind stocks' own past realized returns up to T-1 without violating the no-leakage rule, since that's just the strategy operating causally in live conditions, not evolution touching the transport partition. Still, rank-only scoring would strip away absolute magnitude information that matters for comparing against SAFE's fixed rate, so I'm leaning toward combining an absolute opportunity score with an evolved context-dependent SAFE hurdle function.\n\nI also realize cross-sectional ranks alone can't carry time-varying exposure information since the mean rank is always 0.5 regardless of market conditions — so I need time-series-normalized stock features (like a stock's own momentum relative to its historical distribution) alongside the cross-sectional ranks to give the system real level information to work with.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the mutation and crossover operators needed: point mutations on constants, feature/operator swaps, subtree insertion/deletion, module duplication, applicability gate mutation, and temporal operators for lag/window selection — making sure future-return horizons stay forbidden as inputs while lookback windows remain fair game. For crossover, I want module-level swaps by family/role, subtree crossover, and exchanges of context-detectors and allocation policies, starting complexity around 8-12 active concepts.\n\nI'm setting up staged complexity growth NEAT-style with innovation tracking and speciation protection, plus soft compute bounds that are logged and only trigger an approval request if the elite population hits the cap too often — keeping engineering limits from silently narrowing the hypothesis space. I also need validity checks: type checking, causality (lags must be non-negative), forbidden-variable checks against ticker/position-age/hold-counters, and finite-output verification on probe data.\n\nFor deduplication, I'm canonicalizing expression trees (sorting commutative args, constant-folding, dropping zero-weight terms, quantizing constants) and hashing that form, plus adding a behavioral fingerprint from fixed training-only probe dates so identical decision paths get cached as duplicates. I'm also sketching a library of strategy-family primitives spanning momentum, breakout, trend, mean reversion, volatility, volume/liquidity, relative strength, and earnings-based signals requiring point-in-time data.\n\nOn the earnings data point — if point-in-time earnings surprises aren't available, the system shouldn't silently drop that family but must flag the incompatibility and request approval. I'm rounding out the family list with market/regime, reversal, pullback, volatility breakout, composite breadth, and sector-relative types, treating these families as descriptive seeds rather than rigid categories since modules can mix primitives freely. Now I need to pin down lifecycle semantics — specifically whether decisions made using data through close T execute at the next open.\n\nI'm settling on signals from T close executing at T+1 open (with price plus spread/slippage), using T+1 close as a sensitivity check rather than the primary assumption. Then for comparing a continuing holding's value against a challenger's entry value, I realize there's a subtlety: switching costs are one-time while edges are per-session rates, so comparing them directly requires defining a horizon over which to amortize that one-time cost.\n\nMy solution is to have the GA evolve a switching hurdle coefficient κ that scales the round-trip cost in the replacement rule, acting as an implicit price of friction rather than a fixed holding period — it doesn't depend on position age and doesn't force any exit, so duration emerges naturally rather than being imposed. I could even make κ state-dependent, varying with volatility or uncertainty, and should also consider adding hysteresis to avoid flip-flopping.\n\nNow I'm thinking through which features legitimately create persistence versus which ones secretly smuggle in a time-based horizon. Trend and breadth persistence are fine because they're genuine state features, and incumbency advantage is fine if it comes purely from avoided friction costs rather than age — but something like \"unrealized gain since entry\" is suspicious since it's anchored to entry date and implicitly encodes elapsed time through the return calculation; similarly, an entry-price-anchored stop-loss isn't technically an age-based exit, but it still references the entry point, so I need to decide whether that crosses the line the spec draws against elapsed-age exits.\n\nThe horizon-free principle really is the test here — if two identical holdings would get different decisions purely because of when they were bought, that violates the \"next dollar\" framing, so entry-anchored features like return-since-entry should require approval rather than being freely allowed. On the other hand, a causal state input like the portfolio's own equity drawdown seems more defensible since it's a known crash-defense mechanism and isn't explicitly forbidden, though it does introduce path-dependency on the strategy's own history, which ties into the broader question about portfolio ecosystem interactions.\n\nI'm leaning conservative here: own-equity drawdown should require approval unless it's already in the frozen feature registry, since adding new input classes expands the hypothesis space even though governance only forbids narrowing. I'll classify portfolio-ecosystem state measurements—exposure, concentration, correlation, factor loading—as required by the Q5 framing, while treating own-equity-path metrics separately.\n\nTurning to shorts, I need to treat them as a distinct side with their own borrow cost: a short position's opportunity value is negative unless it clears borrow plus hurdle, so shorts can't be smuggled in as a \"free hedge.\" I'm defining gross exposure as the sum of absolute weights capped at 100%, with the SAFE allocation absorbing undeployed cash, and short proceeds realistically held as collateral earning rebate net of borrow cost.\n\nTo keep things conservative and avoid hidden leverage, I'll say SAFE yield only applies to equity minus long notional minus short notional — treating short margin as fully consuming equity with no interest credited on proceeds, which I can later relax with a sensitivity check using the rebate rate.\n\nFor the allocation mechanics, I want a unified rule: each asset, including SAFE, gets a claim weight, and the final allocation is each claim divided by the sum of all claims. In \"equal\" mode every asset that clears the SAFE hurdle gets a claim of one, while the total risky exposure versus SAFE emerges from comparing aggregate excess opportunity to a SAFE claim term.\n\nI'm also adding a concentration gene: raising all claims to a power γ (which the GA evolves) lets concentration emerge naturally, so high-conviction portfolios like OmniFunds can arise without hardcoded caps — this exponent is irrelevant in equal mode, which is fine. SAFE needs to be treated as a genuine competing candidate, so its excess-opportunity claim must be computed in the same units as the risky assets, measured relative to the SAFE rate itself.\n\nI should stress-test this with synthetic environments: if no asset beats SAFE on a risk-adjusted basis, the strategy should allocate 100% to SAFE; I'll build scenarios where all stocks have negative drift (forcing full SAFE allocation) and where the SAFE rate is unusually large. I also want to guard against initialization bias by seeding the GA population with a range of baseline exposures rather than always-invested genomes, and add diagnostics comparing against an exposure-matched and beta-matched passive benchmark — any promoted strategy must beat these on CAGR at equal or lower drawdown, not just win by holding more exposure.\n\nI'm keeping CAGR vs MDD as the frozen primary Pareto objective rather than adding a third one, but I can introduce guards as feasibility/selection constraints with pre-frozen thresholds — these need explicit sign-off before the run, so I'll mark proposed default values as requiring freeze approval. I also need to pin down exactly how CAGR and MDD get computed during training, whether that's across the four reset eras individually or continuously.\n\nFor robustness, I'm proposing that fitness be computed as a continuous-run CAGR and worst-era MDD over the training-80 split, plus an era-stability guard tracking minimum per-era CAGR. To guard against overfitting to the specific stock basket, I want to add inner cross-validation within the training partition itself — resampling the training-80 into multiple inner stock subsets (e.g., 5 resamples of 64-stock sub-universes) and aggregating fitness across them, which penalizes solutions that depend too heavily on particular stocks.\n\nI should also check whether fitness is evaluated only on the training-80 universe while blind testing uses the full blind-37 set, since the difference in universe size could affect diversification and concentration — this is worth flagging as a diagnostic. For measuring the effective number of independent bets, I'm thinking about using block bootstrap, Newey-West adjusted t-stats clustered by date and stock, and an eigenvalue participation ratio of holdings to estimate true degrees of freedom in the selection process.\n\nCounting distinct major decline/recovery episodes over the past 20 years gives roughly 6-8 events — GFC, 2011, 2015-16, Q4 2018, 2020 COVID crash, 2022 bear market — and I should be careful about referencing anything post-2025 without just citing what's in the packet. Given how few episodes exist, any crash-preservation claims really need per-episode breakdowns and leave-one-episode-out validation rather than pooled statistics.\n\nFor uncertainty quantification, I'd use a stationary block bootstrap on daily returns with blocks of 20-60 sessions to get confidence intervals on CAGR and max drawdown, combined with stock-level resampling and a deflated Sharpe ratio or multiple-testing correction given roughly 130k unique evaluations across the 32k runs times four. With that many evaluations, best-of selection bias becomes massive, so I should apply deflated metrics, a White's reality check or Hansen SPA-style adjustment, and estimate the probability of backtest overfitting via CSCV.\n\nOn fold usage, there are four folds with a predetermined 80/37 split, and the blind 37 in each fold can't be allowed to influence evolution within that fold. But selecting finalists by comparing blind performance across folds turns the blind set itself into a selection mechanism, which is a problem I need to address.\n\nThe fix is to pre-register a selection rule: within each fold, choose finalists purely from training data using something like the training-Pareto candidate with the best lower-confidence-bound CAGR under the guardrails, then evaluate the blind 37 just once on those pre-chosen candidates per band per fold. The blind results across folds then feed into a single go/no-go call on the overall architecture, not into picking individual genomes. For the final frozen genome, I'd either re-evolve on the full DEV117 using the now-validated procedure and apply the same pre-registered training rule, or take a consensus across fold finalists as a diagnostic check for structural recurrence -- I'd lean toward the former as primary validation and the latter as a secondary sanity check.\n\nI also have to admit that repeatedly redesigning after seeing blind results has already started turning DEV117 blind into de facto development data, and this very review is informed by negative blind diagnostics, so I need to log every \"look\" at DEV117 and cap how many are allowed, with the advancement threshold fixed in advance -- precisely why DV25 has to stay sealed as the real test. Separately, since the four folds share 80 of 117 training stocks each, their 37-stock blind sets necessarily overlap too, meaning the folds aren't independent and the effective number of confirmations is less than four. I'm now working out stricter advancement criteria to avoid overfitting to a lucky result.\n\nThe 8-10% CAGR/20% MDD target clearly fails, so I need a principled economic benchmark instead of an arbitrary one -- anchoring to passive alternatives like SPY buy-and-hold (~10-11% CAGR, ~-55% MDD in the GFC) or a risk-matched SPY/T-bill blend. But I realize a mix scaled to 20% MDD might only yield 5-6% CAGR, which would make the current 8-10%/20% target look good by comparison -- so I need an absolute criterion tied directly to the MTS's actual objective rather than a relative passive-beating one.\n\nGiven the user flagged the current output as economically unacceptable, and the project's own prior 50-stock GA development hit ~25-26% CAGR at ~-37% MDD, I'm proposing tiered minimum bars: a CAGR-to-drawdown ratio of at least 0.75 combined with absolute CAGR floors per MDD band (e.g., ≥15% CAGR at ≤20% MDD, scaling up to ≥24% at ≤40% MDD). I'm grounding these floors in the project's own prior development frontier and a dominance-over-passive requirement, explicitly avoiding fitting the threshold to the currently weak observed result.\n\nTesting the ratio idea against a floor of max(15%, 0.6×|MDD|), I check it at the 40% band and against the prior frontier result of 26% CAGR at 37% MDD, which gives a 0.70 ratio — just under my 0.75 threshold. I'm debating whether those in-sample-ish dev results should pass, since they'd likely shrink in live performance anyway, and lean toward building a band table with minimum baseline thresholds instead.\n\nI'm working out a rising CAGR floor by drawdown band, plus requiring outperformance versus SPY's actual total-return CAGR and exposure-matched passive benchmarks by at least 4 percentage points, with MDD also better than SPY's. I'm also considering a bootstrap stability check, requiring the 90% lower confidence bound on CAGR to clear 10%.\n\nFor era-level robustness, I want every era to post positive CAGR with none falling below a minimum threshold, plus at least 3 of 4 folds passing individually and a positive selection diagnostic showing the edge isn't purely beta exposure. On costs, I'm leaning toward requiring the strategy to clear all floors at 10 bps while still retaining most of its excess return over SPY even at 20 bps.\n\nFor crash behavior, I want MTS losses capped at roughly half of SPY's loss during major peak-to-trough declines, with recovery back to prior highs within a reasonable window, and I'll track recovery participation as a measured diagnostic rather than a hard trading rule. I need to be explicit that all these thresholds are my proposal, not derived from the current weak result — they must be reviewed and frozen by the user before any blind redesigned test is run, and I'm just using the current result to illustrate how the criteria would apply (it fails them).\n\nFor the DV25 stage, I'm defining burn criteria: all conformance gates passed, independent audit passed, DEV117 advancement achieved, and the freeze manifest hashed, with a pre-registered pass/fail rule defined before DV25 is opened. I'm weighing whether DV25's bar should be slightly lower than DEV117's to account for expected performance shrinkage, but I'd rather keep the same absolute economic floor and let pre-registration dictate any shrinkage allowance rather than build it in implicitly.\n\nI also want to figure out where the OmniFunds comparison fits best — probably as a concise subsection within A, since it ties into the root-cause architectural discussion rather than standing alone. And for the preflight gates in section K, I'm sketching out machine-testable checks with IDs, test methods, and pass conditions, starting with architecture layer presence checks.\n\nThese need an influence test — perturbing market features must shift portfolio exposure for at least some genomes, which is exactly what would catch the rank-invariance bug. I'm also drafting checks for sector layer influence, enumerating the four allocation modes to confirm each produces distinct weights on the fixture, verifying SPY is loaded from the actual file with a checksum, and an identity check ensuring the comparator isn't just an equal-weight mean — though I should avoid citing specific historical SPY return figures unless I'm certain they're accurate.\n\nI'm planning cost-sensitivity tests: running the fixture at 0/5/10/20 basis points and asserting net return decreases monotonically as turnover increases. For sealed data protection, I'm designing a filesystem sandbox where the runner process lacks read permission to sealed paths, with ticker-list hash checks, a deny-list, syscall/IO audit logging, and canary files in sealed directories that trigger an abort on any access. I'm also considering breadth universe provenance and a time-travel test — truncating data at T and recomputing features to catch future leakage.\n\nNow I'm sketching hold-time tests: a static scan for forbidden identifiers plus a dynamic test confirming that permuting position-entry dates doesn't alter decisions, and that incumbent vs. challenger with identical scores except age yield identical outcomes. For slot/sector caps, I want fixtures showing concentration is allowed when one stock dominates opportunities or when all top stocks cluster in one sector. For the SAFE candidate logic, I'm testing an all-negative-drift fixture (should go 100% SAFE), a fixture where SAFE's rate beats everything else (should also go SAFE), and verifying the SAFE rate lookup is causal.\n\nBeyond that, I need unit tests comparing hand-computed equity curves against CAGR, MDD, CVaR5, turnover (one-way, summed absolute weight changes over two per session including SAFE), and SAFE fraction — plus a consistency check that the same genome evaluated through both the training and blind code paths produces identical metrics, to catch that suspicious 0.0001988 artifact. I also want a turnover sentinel flagging degenerate cases where turnover equals SAFE fraction equals one over T, determinism checks confirming bitwise-identical replay across seeds and worker counts, a rank-invariance test confirming market features genuinely influence decisions, and a check that gross exposure never exceeds one anywhere.\n\nI'm also planning ticker identity checks (shuffling ticker labels or permuting stock order should yield identical portfolios up to relabeling), a spec traceability matrix mapping every frozen requirement to its code location and test ID, and a staged evolution schedule: Stage 0 for conformance and non-tuned baselines like SPY and equal-weight, Stage 1 for discovering strategy modules per family niche using island populations with migration, Stage 2 for co-evolving context/applicability gates over market and sector conditions, and Stage 3 for the full ecosystem covering allocation, SAFE, shorting, and replacement.\n\nNow I'm thinking through Stage 4 for robustness refinement and finalist selection using training-only inner resampling, making sure staging only shapes initialization and operator probabilities rather than freezing earlier components. For population sizing, I'm weighing options like 6-8 islands of ~250 genomes each against larger scales, factoring in generation counts with plateau-based extension, and considering multi-fidelity screening on date/stock subsamples to control the heavy compute cost of millions of evaluations per fold.\n\nBut I need multi-fidelity screening to never bias against rarely-acting strategies like crash defense -- so low-fidelity subsets must stratify in all decline/recovery episodes, and I'd only allow multi-fidelity if rank-correlation against full fidelity passes a threshold and never eliminates crash-defense niche candidates. I'm also designing a MAP-Elites style archive over behavioral descriptors (exposure, SAFE fraction, short share, turnover, concentration, crash-episode return) plus novelty search and drawdown-banded islands to keep the population from collapsing into always-invested solutions.\n\nEach drawdown-band island uses an epsilon-constraint approach, maximizing CAGR subject to that band's MDD cap, giving dedicated search pressure to underperforming bands like the 10% one, combined with global NSGA-II/III search. Seeding is fully deterministic with hashed per-fold, per-island seeds, checkpointed RNG state for bitwise-reproducible resumption, and plateau detection via hypervolume improvement thresholds that first boost mutation diversity, then halt islands on a second plateau -- with compute expansion only permitted under pre-registered rules.\n\nParallel genome evaluation uses deterministic reduction and hash-keyed results. For the fitness function, I'm now weighing how to model realistic transaction costs -- using a liquidity-based spread/slippage estimate (like Corwin-Schultz or bps-by-volume tier) rather than a flat 5bps during evolution, while reserving the 0/5/10/20 bps sensitivity sweep for finalist evaluation, and leaning toward evolving at a conservative cost level so the GA isn't overfit to unrealistically cheap trading.\n\nI'm settling on: evolution cost uses the full realistic model (liquidity-based spread, slippage, SEC fee, FINRA TAF, borrow cost), with a finalist-stage guard requiring survival at a 10 bps training-only stress test rather than adding extra constraints mid-evolution. For max drawdown, I'm defining the objective as the worst drawdown across the continuous run, while also tracking era-level MDD separately to catch issues masked by era resets.\n\nFor aggregation across inner resamples, I'm weighing whether to compute objectives as a median across several training sub-universes versus using the full training set directly.\n\nI'll settle on keeping the primary objectives strictly CAGR and MDD computed on the full training-80, with the inner-resample lower-bound serving only as a guard and tie-breaker within an epsilon margin, rather than redefining the objectives themselves.\n\nFor guards, I want feasibility-style constraints: no leverage beyond gross exposure of 1, an era-level guard ensuring each training era beats the safe/Treasury return by some margin, and tail-risk guards capping worst single-day and worst 20-session drawdowns.\n\nI'm also considering a recovery-time cap (underwater no more than ~3 years), a turnover/cost-stress guard to penalize pathological trading, and an effective-evidence guard so the strategy's CAGR isn't dependent on just a handful of stocks via leave-top-contributor-out testing.\n\nThere's a key distinction here between allocation concentration, which is fine, and evidence concentration, which signals overfitting and needs guarding. I'm also thinking about a selection-skill guard, requiring training selected-minus-unselected performance to beat exposure-matched passive returns so the edge isn't just beta, plus inner-resample stability checks across subsets of stocks, with everything else serving as diagnostics. All thresholds need to be locked in before running anything.\n\nOnly apply the complexity tie-breaker when results are within uncertainty bounds, using epsilon-dominance derived from bootstrap standard errors. I want to guard against high-exposure bias through a behavior archive and exposure-matched benchmarks, judging competing approaches on absolute scores. I also want crash-specific metrics like downside capture ratio, recovery capture after troughs, and a \"recovery burden\" combining drawdown depth and duration similar to an ulcer index. For features, I'm keeping the existing cross-sectional rank setup but adding time-series normalized versions like volatility-scaled returns.\n\nI should expand sector features substantially: ETF returns across multiple windows, relative strength versus SPY, breadth, drawdown, volatility, dispersion, stock-versus-sector residuals, momentum ranking across sectors, stress, concentration of leadership, and correlation to market, all with a point-in-time sector mapping. I also want to add moving-average geometry like gaps and slopes at the stock level, plus positive-day fractions and failed rebound indicators.\n\nI'm also thinking through distance to 52-week highs, Donchian channels, ATR, volatility compression ratios, beta, idiosyncratic volatility, residual momentum, liquidity measures like dollar volume and Amihud illiquidity, overnight versus intraday gaps, skewness, lottery-style max daily returns, short-term reversal, and earnings timing as point-in-time stock state features. At the market level I'm considering trend geometry for SPY, VIX term structure, T-bill rates, yield curve spread, credit spreads, correlation and dispersion measures, and cross-sectional breadth statistics.\n\nI'm weighing temporal operators like deltas, acceleration, persistence counts, and rolling max/min, including causal \"sessions since trough\" style features — these are market-state timing measurements rather than position-age tracking, so they're permissible as long as they're not hard-coded into fixed rules. The key distinction I need to maintain is between derived measurements computed deterministically from past data versus actual trading rules that map to positions, since only the genetic algorithm should be evolving the latter.\n\nFor normalization, I'm deciding to use within-universe percentile ranks rather than raw ranks, since rank distributions shift with universe size across training and blind sets. I also need to make sure expanding-window time-series percentiles have proper warmup data starting before the 2006-09-15 era boundary, so no feature ever leaks future information relative to T.\n\nNow I'm outlining the per-session decision algorithm: at time T, compute market, sector, and stock-level context vectors, then for each stock and module calculate applicability, signal, uncertainty, and downside scores, and aggregate them into an opportunity score weighted across modules.\n\nFor deriving the long-side value from opportunity, I'm weighing whether to subtract a safe-rate hurdle directly or instead use an online calibration approach — mapping raw opportunity scores into expected next-session excess returns via isotonic or linear calibration fit on the strategy's own historical (opportunity, realized return) pairs, since mixing scales directly seems problematic.\n\nThis keeps things causally legal: training calibration draws from training stocks' past, blind replay draws from blind stocks' own past returns up to the prior session, which mirrors how a live system would calibrate against its own universe history rather than leaking future information. The calibration window itself (expanding vs half-life) becomes an evolvable allele, since a short half-life risks conflating with holding-period length.\n\nI'm simplifying toward requiring opportunity values be expressed as an estimated incremental excess return rate per session — letting the GA learn an affine mapping (scale/offset) from raw evolved scores to this common currency, since an evolved hurdle is mathematically equivalent and the online calibration just adds an empirical anchor so SAFE competes in identical units.\n\nFor incumbent positions, there's no inherent loyalty bonus—switching from one position to another only happens if the gain exceeds exit and entry friction costs plus an uncertainty penalty, with that threshold evolved and state-dependent rather than fixed by holding duration. Exits to SAFE trigger when a position's excess return turns negative enough to outweigh exit friction, and shorts follow a mirrored formula incorporating borrow costs rather than interest earned on proceeds.\n\nNow I'm working out allocation: each qualifying position claims a share proportional to some power of its adjusted excess (equal, raw strength, uncertainty-adjusted, or downside-adjusted depending on mode), and I need to figure out how the total risky budget is set against SAFE's claim—since SAFE has zero excess return by definition, it needs its own evolved claim function based on market risk context rather than competing directly on excess value.\n\nA cleaner way to frame this: think of it like target portfolio weights proportional to risk-adjusted excess return for each asset, scaled down if the sum exceeds 1, with any leftover automatically flowing to SAFE—so when few opportunities look good or risk is high, SAFE naturally absorbs the remainder without needing a contrived separate formula.\n\nI'm shaping this as a raw risky allocation per asset that's the product of an evolved risk-appetite scaler (state-dependent, letting crash-defense behavior emerge rather than being hardcoded) and a mode-specific signal function—equal mode just flags positive-expectation assets, strength mode uses raw expectation, uncertainty mode divides by uncertainty, and downside mode divides by downside risk.\n\nWith an optional sharpness exponent applied, I'd sum the absolute values of all raw claims and rescale down to fit within a gross exposure of 1 if needed, letting SAFE absorb the rest—since these claims live in absolute units rather than already summing to one, this naturally lets concentration and cash-holding emerge from the mode and sharpness choices. I also want a no-trade band based on transaction friction, so tiny rebalances below some cost-derived threshold get skipped entirely, keeping this state-based rather than tied to any fixed horizon.\n\nFor reporting, I need to tag each portfolio change with a decision type: ENTER for new positions, CONTINUE for holds (including resized ones), REPLACE when one position's edge decisively exceeds another's within the same session, EXIT_TO_SAFE when proceeds move to cash, and SHORT for negative exposure—so I'll fold partial adds/trims into CONTINUE with resizing, then sequence execution as compute targets, apply no-trade/hysteresis filters, then execute. For shorts, I need gross exposure to include the short magnitude, and I'm working out how SAFE/cash accrual should treat short proceeds conservatively, treating margin collateral as reducing available safe equity rather than assuming full interest-bearing cash.\n\nI also want to measure concentration via HHI, effective N, max weight, sector HHI, and factor betas, and I'm weighing whether to add a covariance-aware allocation mode—but since only four modes were approved, that would need separate sign-off, so I'll just flag it as a future candidate. For portfolio-ecosystem interactions, I'll fold correlation with existing holdings into the downside/uncertainty adjustment as an input feature rather than building a separate module.\n\nFor Q5, I'm leaning toward yes on treating strategy modules, conditional logic, applicability, sector-relative, and temporal morphology as first-class since they fit the approved architecture, while portfolio-ecosystem effects should enter as inputs plus an optional allele—but since that alters the definition of an already-approved allocation mode, it needs explicit approval and should default to off.\n\nNow I'm working through the economics side: need to decide whether to use total-return SPY (dividends reinvested) as the benchmark to match MTS's adjusted-close stock returns, and specify this consistency explicitly. For the risk-free rate, I'm considering FRED's DTB3 three-month T-bill series, converting from discount basis to bond-equivalent yield, and accounting for its one-day publication lag in the H.15 release.\n\nI also need to nail down trading costs: spread estimates by liquidity tier, SEC Section 31 fees on sells, FINRA TAF, and borrow costs -- assuming general collateral rates around 0.25-0.5% annualized for easy-to-borrow names, with sensitivity analysis at 0.5%, 2%, and 5% for hard-to-borrow scenarios given uncertain historical locate availability. On execution, I'm assuming fills happen at next open with half-spread plus slippage, acknowledging gaps and halts as unresolved complications.\n\nDelisted stocks raise a survivorship bias concern -- if the DEV117 universe wasn't selected point-in-time, results could be biased upward, so I need to flag this as an audit item requiring documentation. I also want strict naming conventions for comparator variables like SPY_TR_actual and EW_DEV_partition_mean, verified against source files, plus clear definitions for decline episodes (10% corrections, 20% bear markets) that are used only for ex-post reporting, not as causal inputs.\n\nFor the per-fold procedure itself, I'm laying out four folds each with an 80-session training set and 37-session blind set drawn from a frozen fold file hash, with stock-level features computed from each stock's own history and cross-sectional ranks calculated separately within training and blind sets, while market-level features must come from external sources rather than the partition stocks unless explicitly approved and documented.\n\nFinalist selection happens strictly on training data via a pre-registered rule, selecting per drawdown band the genome with the highest training CAGR lower-confidence-bound that satisfies the band's max training drawdown and passes guards, breaking ties by complexity, with results hashed and frozen before any blind evaluation occurs. Blind evaluation then happens exactly once on the frozen finalists, with no re-selection permitted, and blind results are written to a separate store that the evolution process cannot read — meaning all four folds' evolution must fully complete before any blind results are unsealed.\n\nThen I aggregate across folds to decide architecture-level go/no-go, examining whether structural motifs or modules recurred across finalists and whether performance was stable. For the final DV25 model, I'm pre-registering two candidate approaches now: either refitting the identical frozen procedure on the full 117-stock universe to produce a frozen genome set validated by the DEV117 blind results, or using a cross-fold consensus/ensemble — leaning toward the full refit as primary, though I note that approach itself wouldn't be separately transport-tested.\n\nGiven multiple-testing concerns, I'd restrict DV25 to a small pre-declared candidate set — at most one candidate per target band, capped at two or three bands total — rather than burning DV25 on many options.\n\nDiagnostics like per-stock transport, era breakdowns, decline tables, and motif recurrence should only inform the go/no-go decision, never feed back into genome selection itself. If a redesign fails DEV117 gates and gets iterated again, that's another look that erodes the blind set's validity, so I should cap further iterations — maybe two more looks at most — and log each one.\n\nRanking discrepancies now, the most critical one: the global linear score converted to cross-sectional rank makes all market and R1 features mathematically inert for selection, since rank is invariant to per-date constants, and it fixes the number of qualifying names by quantile regardless of exposure state. This single design choice could eliminate crash-defense, R1 discovery, regime-conditioned behavior, and SAFE competition — likely explaining why SAFE scores near zero.\n\nChecking further: if strength is derived as rank minus qualification threshold and weights are gross-normalized to ≤1, the portfolio stays fully invested whenever any strength is positive, meaning SAFE margin is either always or never binding against a rank-based distribution — so it essentially never triggers. I'm also flagging the absence of any sector layer, and the absence of any strategy-family, applicability, or conditional structure, since a single global linear model can't represent that kind of conditional logic.\n\nThat means SAFE isn't a genuine competitor at all — it's a direct consequence of the rank-scoring setup, with measured SAFE converging near 1/T. The turnover/SAFE metric landing near 1/5030 looks suspicious too, almost identical across runs, which points to either a metric artifact or an initialization-only event; I need to root-cause whether this reflects a \"day-one basket\" degeneracy where the GA never replaces its initial picks, effectively doing stock selection via ticker identity rather than learned signal. I'm also noting the comparator mislabels SPY as the equal-weight mean of DEV117, which may improperly include the blind37 stocks.\n\nI need to confirm this isn't leaking into any feature or decline-episode definition used by evolution, since that would make the reporting invalid. The cost model is a flat hard-coded 5bps with no sensitivity testing and no regulatory or borrow costs, and given near-zero training turnover, cost pressure barely matters anyway. Equal allocation was omitted entirely, which is a spec violation with moderate scientific consequence since equal weighting is often a robust baseline. I'm also flagging that stock features are purely cross-sectional ranks with no time-series or absolute state, making time-varying conviction impossible — combined with the earlier design flaw, this bumps up to a high-severity issue. There's also unresolved provenance questions around the market feature's universe, possible cross-partition contamination, and short-selling mechanics that need auditing.\n\nI'm noting the short mechanics use a symmetric boolean inverse-rank scheme that forces an always-on short book with no borrow costs or state-conditioned applicability. Finalist selection via blind band display risks selection bias since whichever candidate appears in the blind frontier gets chosen, which needs pre-registration to fix. I suspect required diagnostics like CVaR5, drawdown velocity, and effective N may be missing from the reporting packet and should be flagged as unverified until tested, while the search budget itself seems adequate and the fixed 8-12 feature count is a moderate concern since it was meant to be provisional, not a permanent ceiling.\n\nGiven that 20 of 28 features turned out inert, it looks like the GA wasted slots on dead weight, though the simplifications like single-file implementation, vectorized rank transforms, causal percentile transforms, and deterministic folds are all reasonable choices. Now I'm cataloguing genuine ambiguities in the approved spec itself — how SAFE competes in units, the friction-vs-rate tradeoff, market breadth universe provenance, cross-fold finalist selection rules, the absence of numeric economic advancement thresholds, drawdown band semantics, training-versus-blind universe size asymmetry, execution timing, and data availability for earnings and sector ETFs.\n\nI also need to flag data-availability issues: VVIX history effectively starts around 2007, so era 1 starting in 2006-09 would be missing that feature, and OFR funding/credit series have varying start dates too — this needs explicit handling for missing features before 2007. Separately, the disappointing economic results (8.85 vs 19.86) are a valid negative result for the reduced runner but don't actually test the approved architecture, and the fact that blind CAGR exceeds training at the 20% candidate suggests partition beta differences rather than real skill, reinforcing that the selection diagnostic shows no genuine selection skill.\n\nI'm also weighing whether CAGR/MDD is the right Pareto objective — MDD is a single-path, high-variance statistic dominated by the GFC episode in training, which incentivizes overfitting to that one crisis; I'll keep CAGR/MDD as the frozen objective but add guards like era-specific MDD and leave-one-episode-out validation. On whether linear rank is fundamentally too weak for this architecture, I conclude yes — not because linear models can't capture alpha, but because rank-invariance strips out context, there's no way to model conditional interactions or absolute levels, and the quantile qualification step artificially constrains investment breadth.\n\nA linear model with interactions and absolute output is really just a special case of the richer genome space, so I should seed it as a baseline module to ensure the new GA's hypothesis space stric